  "response": "Let's break down the requirements and constraints step by step, then construct a feasible schedule in JSON format as requested.\n\n### **Restating the Events and Constraints**\n\n#### **Events**\n1. **A: Gym** — 07:00 to 10:00 (60 min)\n2. **B: Supermarket** — Must be attended between 09:30 and 18:00, duration 40 min\n3. **C: Home** — Two consecutive 60-min periods, must end before event D starts\n4. **D: Restaurant** — Fixed time slot 12:30-14:00 (1.5 hours)\n5. **E: Library** — 30 min session starting between 14:00 and 17:00\n6. **F: Home** — 45-min period ending before 20:00\n\n#### **Constraints**\n1. After finishing A (ends at 10:00), there must be a 30-min recovery immediately afterward → Recovery from 10:00 to 10:30.\n2. B (supermarket) must start between 09:30 and 18:00, last 40 min ⇒ possible end times: 10:10–17:40\n3. C is two consecutive 60-min home blocks, both before D starts (D is 12:30-14:00). So the first block ends no later than 12:30.\n4. D is fixed at 12:30-14:00.\n5. E (library) must start between 14:00 and 17:00 and last 30 min ⇒ can start anytime from 14:00 to 16:30\n6. F (home, 45 min) must end before 20:00 ⇒ starts no later than 19:15\n\n7. **A/F are not adjacent** — The A event ends at 10:00; the next F block cannot start immediately after (i.e., can't start at 10:00 or later). So, if F is scheduled after 10:30, that's fine.\n\n8. **12:00–13:00 cannot be used for travel** — No movement/travel allowed in this time slot\n\n---\n\n## **Constructing a Feasible Schedule**\n\nLet’s try to build one step by step:\n\n### Step 1: Gym and Recovery\n- Gym: 07:00–10:00 (A)\n- Immediate recovery: 10:00–10:30\n\n### Step 2: Supermarket (B) must start after 09:30, so earliest is 09:30. But we need to fit C before 12:30.\n\nLet’s schedule C first as two home blocks ending before 12:30:\n\nOption:\n- Home Block 1: 08:00–09:00\n- Home Block 2: 09:00–10:00 (but gym is from 07:00–10:00, so overlap with gym. Not allowed.)\n\nAlternative:\nLet’s shift C after Gym but before D.\n\nSuppose we do A (gym) 07:00–10:00  \nRecovery 10:00–10:30\n\nNow, let's schedule B (supermarket). It must start no earlier than 09:30 and finish by 17:40 at latest. Let’s pick 11:10–11:50 for B.\n\nThen C needs to be two consecutive 60-min home slots before D starts (12:30). So the last slot of C must end before 12:30.\n\nLet’s try:\n- Home Block 1: 10:30–11:30\n- Home Block 2: 11:30–12:30 (ends exactly at 12:30, so just before D starts)\n\nBut after A ends at 10:00 and recovery is until 10:30, we can start C.\n\nLet’s do:\n- Home Block 1: 10:30–11:30\n- Home Block 2: 11:30–12:30 (so both are consecutive and before D)\n\nNow, after 12:30, D starts at 12:30–14:00. That works.\n\nNext, E (library) must start between 14:00 and 17:00 for a 30-min slot:\nLet’s pick 14:30–15:00\n\nF (home, 45 min) must end before 20:00 ⇒ latest start is 19:15.\nWe can place F after library or elsewhere.\n\nA/F not adjacent: A ends at 10:00. Next F block cannot start at 10:00 or later. So if we schedule F from e.g. 16:15–17:00, that's fine (after E).\n\nLet’s lay out the full timeline:\n\n| Time          | Event         |\n|---------------|--------------|\n| 07:00–10:00   | Gym (A)      |\n| 10:00–10:30   | Recovery     |\n| 10:30–11:30   | Home (C1)    |\n| 11:30–12:30   | Home (C2)    |\n| 12:30–14:00   | Restaurant (D)|\n| 14:30–15:00   | Library (E)  |\n| 15:00–15:45   | Free time    |\n| 16:00–16:45   | Free time    |\n| 16:15–17:00   | Home (F)     |\n| 17:00–20:00+  | Return home, rest |\n\nBut F must be a single 45-min block ending before 20:00. So 16:15–17:00 works.\n\nCheck constraints:\n- B is from 11:10–11:50 (within 09:30–18:00)\n- C blocks are 10:30–12:30, both consecutive and end at 12:30 before D\n- E is 14:30–15:00 (in allowed window)\n- F is 16:15–17:00 (ends by 17:00 <20:00)\n- A ends at 10:00; next F starts at 16:15 → not adjacent\n- 12:00–13:00 is free, no travel scheduled\n\nThis schedule works.\n\nNow let's output the JSON as requested. The user wants a feasible solution in this format:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"events\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Gym\", \"start\": \"07:00\", \"end\": \"10:00\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Recovery\", \"start\": \"10:00\", \"end\": \"10:30\"},   // Not listed as an event in the original, but implied by constraint. If only main events are needed, maybe omit this?\n    {\"name\": \"Home\", \"start\": \"10:30\", \"end\": \"11:30\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Home\", \"start\": \"11:30\", \"end\": \"12:30\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Restaurant\", \"start\": \"12:30\", \"end\": \"14:00\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Library\", \"start\": \"14:30\", \"end\": \"15:00\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Home\", \"start\": \"16:15\", \"end\": \"17:00\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nBut since the original events are only A (gym), B (supermarket), C (home blocks), D (restaurant), E (library), F (home). The recovery is a consequence, not an explicit event. So perhaps we should only list the named events and infer the order.\n\nAlternatively, if you want to include all time-blocks as \"events\" for clarity:\n\nBut per user's instruction: \"output JSON feasible/events\", likely expecting the main events with their times.\n\nLet me structure it using just the original event names (A-F), but showing their scheduled slots. However, C is two consecutive home periods before D starts — so we can represent them as separate time intervals for C.\n\nHere’s a possible JSON representation of one feasible schedule:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"events\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Gym\", \"start\": \"07:00\", \"end\": \"10:00\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Supermarket\", \"start\": \"11:10\", \"end\": \"11:50\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Home\", \"start\": \"10:30\", \"end\": \"12:30\"},  // First home block (C1)\n    {\"name\": \"Home\", \"start\": \"11:30\", \"end\": \"12:30\"}, // Second home block (C2), but these are consecutive so maybe merge or clarify as part of C\n    {\"name\": \"Restaurant\", \"start\": \"12:30\", \"end\": \"14:00\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Library\", \"start\": \"14:30\", \"end\": \"15:00\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Home\", \"start\": \"16:15\", \"end\": \"17:00\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nBut to strictly follow the original event definitions (A-F), and since C is two consecutive home periods before D, perhaps we should represent C as a single entry with start/end times indicating it's two blocks:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"events\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Gym\", \"start\": \"07:00\", \"end\": \"10:00\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Supermarket\", \"start\": \"11:10\", \"end\": \"11:50\"}, // B\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Home (C)\",\n      \"intervals\": [\n        {\"start\": \"10:30\", \"end\": \"11:30\"},\n        {\"start\": \"11:30\", \"end\": \"12:30\"}\n      ]\n    },\n    {\"name\": \"Restaurant\", \"start\": \"12:30\", \"end\": \"14:00\"}, // D\n    {\"name\": \"Library\", \"start\": \"14:30\", \"end\": \"15:00\"}, // E\n    {\"name\": \"Home (F)\", \"start\": \"16:15\", \"end\": \"17:00\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThis format clearly shows all constraints are satisfied and A/F are not adjacent. The 12:00–13:00 slot is empty, so travel isn't needed there.\n\n**Final JSON Feasible Schedule (one possible answer):**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"events\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Gym\", \"start\": \"07:00\", \"end\": \"10:00\"},\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Home (C1)\",\n      \"start\": \"10:30\",\n      \"end\": \"11:30\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Home (C2)\",\n      \"start\": \"11:30\",\n      \"end\": \"12:30\"\n    },\n    {\"name\": \"Restaurant\", \"start\": \"12:30\", \"end\": \"14:00\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Library\", \"start\": \"14:30\", \"end\": \"15:00\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Home (F)\", \"start\": \"16:15\", \"end\": \"17:00\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThis schedule satisfies all constraints and is a valid feasible solution.",
